{
  "case_id": "2020-19-968",
  "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When Georgia Gwinnett officials stopped Chike Uzuegbunam and Joseph Bradford from sharing their faith, the officials caused concrete injuries. Chike and Joseph lost forever the chance to get those days back and speak their message to their peers. No policy change can ever restore that lost opportunity. And as this Court said in Carey, Stachura, and Farrar, the appropriate remedy to redress those past harms is nominal damages. Nominal damages awards satisfy Article III. Farrar explains that nominal damages provide relief on the merits, vindicate the plaintiff through an enforceable judgment, and"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Eleventh Circuit's outlier rule is a radical departure. For centuries, English and American courts have awarded nominal damages when no future threat exists, even after a plaintiff waives compensatory damages. Every circuit to address the issue does the same, even the Eleventh, until this recent decision. This Court should retain the long-standing rule. It has not resulted in a glut of cases, and the alternative makes a mess of this Court's clear Article III jurisprudence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So we have said -- and this is somewhat a different version or similar to the Chief Justice's concern -- we've said that -- that an injury has to be real and substantial. But, if you're only asking for a --a dollar or nominal damages, doesn't that seem to undermine the real and substantial requirement?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Good morning. Well, as -- as you are aware, Congress passes lots of statutes and they have tens of thousands of words, and people frequently think that one new set of words is -- is unconstitutional at least as enforced. We're not supposed to give advisory opinions. But, if somebody comes in in the con -- course of conduct under the statute or what they're going to follow, why -- why -- is not going to be done anymore. It's the same question as the Chief's: Why -- why isn't that just an advisory opinion?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0000-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You have said that nominal damages serve to vindicate a past violation of a constitutional right. And it would be helpful to me if you could perhaps explain more specifically what you mean by the vindication of a constitutional right that was violated."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0000-0010",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "required more than nominal damages for standing. They point out that your client initially had a compensatory damages claim as part of this lawsuit. Why aren't they right? Perhaps your client has scruples against seeking more than a dollar and others might as well, but why should the law care about that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Hashim M. Mooppan",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioners suffered an unquestionable Article III injury when Respondents censored their speech, and Petitioners seek the paradigmatic type of Article III redress for that past injury, a tangible award of money."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are a -- a series of cases they --they cite at pages 34 to 35 of their brief where common law courts, appellate courts, said it was harmless error to not have awarded nominal damages precisely because there wasn't a likelihood of future harm."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Respondents emphasize harmless, but the real key is that error. The appellate courts there recognized it was error not to award nominal damages even though there was no likelihood of future harm."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Okay. But, if you have nominal damages, you can get attorney's fees potentially, correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "At bottom, the question whether nominal damages resists mootness in a case like this one reduces to the question whether nominal damages redress past injuries."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: What?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Pinson",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 75,
        "start": 75
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PINSON: They are symbolic of the fact that a plaintiff has proved a legal violation but is entitled to zero compensation for it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "states' amicus brief",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0003-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 84,
        "start": 84
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "states' amicus brief at 14 through 18"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 85,
        "start": 85
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What's the -- what's the problem with that approach?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Pinson",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0004-0001",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 85,
        "start": 85
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "it -- it's not so clear to me that that judgment wouldn't have not only preclusive effect but -- but other effects."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0004-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 86,
        "start": 86
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Okay. And then, on the attorney's fees question that's been raised, those -- if those were fully available, then that would provide, obviously, an incentive for some plaintiffs to continue litigating even if there was no other injunctive or compensatory relief at stake."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Pinson",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0004-0003",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yes. So I do think that the better reading of Farrar is that generally you shouldn't get any attorney's fees for a nominal damages award."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0004-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 92,
        "start": 92
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But the Petitioners here haven't suffered such a slight injury?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Waggoner",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2ff1619251b5c5f165a3897e691b5cd4f8d768ce917274e93dc0caf96ca74b5d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2ff1619251b5-0004-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 94,
        "start": 94
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Partial redress is still redress. This Court has held that rule over and over again."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2020-19-968",
      "confidence": 0.0,
